\begin{abstract}
We present a time-resolved analysis of \textit{Chandra} High Energy Transmission Grating Spectrometer (HETGS) observations of the high-mass X-ray binary Cygnus~X-1, focusing on rapid variability in its highly ionized absorption-line spectrum. Resonance lines from H-like and He-like ions exhibit pronounced changes in equivalent width, centroid velocity, and profile morphology on timescales much shorter than the binary orbital period. In several intervals, strong absorption features weaken or disappear and subsequently re-emerge, accompanied by changes in line asymmetry and velocity structure. These variations cannot be explained solely by fluctuations in the broadband continuum. Instead, they indicate rapid evolution of the density, ionization state, and line-of-sight velocity field of the focused stellar wind from the supergiant companion. The inferred timescales and correlated behavior among ions favor a structured, clumpy wind containing overdense parcels embedded in a more highly ionized medium, with additional modulation arising from X-ray photoionization near the compact object. Our results demonstrate that time-averaged spectra obscure substantial dynamical complexity and establish high-cadence grating spectroscopy as a powerful probe of wind accretion, photoionization, and mass transfer in Cygnus~X-1.
\end{abstract}
